    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 45210,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45210.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Well, again, I think over the years developed much deeper devotion to the institutions that resolve the differences that we have as a people. And we're in a period right now where we're seeing very, very intense differences. But we need to bear in mind that we do have the best institutions ever designed to resolve such differences as long as we're talking with each other as the American people rather than shouting at each other. And as the years have gone by, I've become more and more convinced of that.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker has developed a deeper devotion to institutions that resolve differences among people.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "There are currently very intense differences among the American people.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The institutions designed to resolve differences are the best ever created.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Constructive dialogue between Americans is necessary for these institutions to function effectively.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker has become increasingly convinced of the value of these institutions over time.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Institutions like courts and legislatures are legitimate mechanisms for resolving societal disagreements.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Dialogue based on mutual respect is more effective than adversarial confrontation.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that the current political climate involves significant polarization.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Effective resolution of differences requires ongoing communication among citizens.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views institutional stability as essential to national cohesion.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 45210,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45210.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "You know, being a successful conservative Republican politician in California is a feat in itself, I think, especially in last year's election, which saw seven Republican seats switch over to Democrats. Obviously, representing the fourth district helps, but what else would you credit in helping you to accomplish multiple reelection wins?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Being a successful conservative Republican politician in California is a notable achievement.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Last year's election resulted in seven Republican seats switching to Democratic control.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The politician has won re-election multiple times.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Representing the fourth congressional district provides some advantage to the politician.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "California is generally a politically liberal state where conservative Republicans face significant challenges.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Electoral success for a Republican in California requires more than just party affiliation; it demands exceptional performance or strategic advantages.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that the politician’s repeated re-election suggests underlying factors beyond district representation.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener is expected to understand the political context of California elections and the significance of seat flips.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Re-election victories by Republicans in California are rare enough to warrant recognition.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 45210,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45210.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, more than anything, I've been very consistent in my message over the years that I've served in public office, and that is that freedom works. The freedom that the American founders recognize comes not from government, but comes from what they call the laws of nature and of nature's God, of our Creator. We're born with those rights. They're not created by government. Rather, we create governments to protect those rights. And it's not just an ideological standpoint, as a practical matter, it has proven over the centuries to produce the happiest and most prosperous and most just societies in the experience of human civilization. I've found that the people of the Fourth Congressional District believe these things very deeply. And I think that's also true of the American people overall.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I have been consistently messaging about freedom during my time in public office.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Freedom works as a principle in society.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The freedoms recognized by the American founders originate from natural law and divine law, not from government.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Individuals are born with inherent rights that predate government.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Governments are established to protect individual rights rather than to create them.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "This view on freedom is supported by historical evidence from centuries of human civilization.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The people of the Fourth Congressional District strongly believe in these principles.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "These principles are widely held among the general American population.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker’s consistency in messaging enhances their credibility and political standing.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Natural rights theory is both philosophically valid and practically effective.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Historical success of free societies provides reliable evidence for the superiority of freedom.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Belief in foundational rights is shared across broad segments of the American electorate.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker holds moral authority to speak on issues of rights and governance.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Audience members value consistency and long-term commitment in political leaders.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "The concept of 'laws of nature and of nature's God' is a legitimate basis for political philosophy.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Freedom as defined here leads to happiness, prosperity, and justice as measurable outcomes.",
