  {
    "episode_id": 45210,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45210.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "Well, first let's just get a brief background of our water situation. Fortunately, we live in one of the most water-rich regions of the country in northern California. But, you know, everybody seems to think that the Colorado River is the great river in the West. Colorado River is a junior sister of the Sacramento. The Sacramento is a much, much larger river. The difference is we store 70 million acre feet of water on the Colorado. We only store 10 million acre feet on the Sacramento and lose most of the rest of that to the Pacific Ocean every year. What I learned, chairing the water committee for four years, is that droughts are nature's fault. They happen. But water shortages are our fault. Water shortages are a result of deliberate choice that we made to stop building dams. And the reason we stop building dams is because of the same environmental laws that have made the management of our forests impossible has also made the construction of new reservoirs cost prohibitive. We have not added a major reservoir over a million acre feet to our water system in California in the last, well, since 1979. Meanwhile, the population has nearly doubled. We're not going to solve our water shortages until we build more dams, and we're not going to build more dams until we overhaul the laws that have made their construction impossible. I'm just to give you one example. I've got the town of Forest Hill in my district. They get their water from a small reservoir called the Sugar Pine Reservoir, built years ago with an 18-foot spillway, but no spillway gate. didn't need the extra water at the time due now. That spillway gate is going to cost that little town about $5,000, $2 million. But that's not the price of the actual project. That's just the price of the gate. To the price of the actual gate, you then have to add another $1 million minimum in environmental studies, $2 million more in environmental mitigations, and $6 million is a forest service fee to move a trail that goes around the lake and about a dozen campsites. So a project that was viable for that little community to increase its water storage becomes a cost prohibitive boondoggle, and that's what stalling all of our projects. So, the act that you're referring to begins to move us back toward prioritizing water storage again, but we've got an awful long way to go. And we're not going to get there until we streamline and overhaul these laws that are making their construction so difficult.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
