  {
    "episode_id": 45210,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45210.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Well, everything you need to know about the federal budget with three numbers, 26, 29, and 46. 26% is the increase of inflation population over the last 10 years. 29% is the increase in revenue. So our revenues, even after the tax cuts, have more than kept pace with inflation and population. In fact, after the tax cuts, we're actually taking in record amounts of new revenue because of the economic expansion that those tax cuts made possible. So 26% increase in inflation and population combined, 29% increase in revenues. Here's what's killing us. That third number, 46% is the increase in federal spending in the same period. We are spending vastly more than the growth of our revenues. And the result is catastrophic deficits. Now accumulated debt of more than $23 trillion. But what's really dangerous about that is the interest costs that we have to pay. We're paying about 400, very rough terms, about $400 billion a year just in interest. That's money going out the door from your tax. And when we talk about $1 billion, by the way, in federal money, Art Laffer calls those MEGO numbers, MEGO, my eyes glaze over. I've always found it important to use some real world standard. Every billion dollars we throw around in Washington, think of that as eight dollars from your family's budget because if you divide the number of families into a billion dollars, it's eight dollars. So when we talk about a trillion dollars, we're talking about eight thousand dollars out of your family's budget. When we talk about running up a one trillion, almost one trillion dollar deficit next year, That's $8,000 being added to your family's credit card bill. And this isn't theoretical. This is just as real as if it appeared on your credit card bill. In fact, it's more real because you're required to pay back that $8,000 before you can pay back the first dollar of your own debt. And the IRS is always very insistent that you do. That comes out of your future taxes. So this is absolutely killing our country. And so when you talk about $400 billion a year, just an interest cost, put that in perspective, our entire defense establishment right now costs about $700 billion. And the growth of the interest payments on that debt is so steep that they estimate within the next few years. We're going to be paying more just to rent money we've already spent then we're able to spend for the entire defense of our country. That is simply not sustainable and history is shouting this warning at us that countries that bankrupt themselves simply aren't around very long because before you can provide for the common defense or promote the general welfare, you've got to be able to pay for it and that debt is now threatening our country's future ability to do so. It's got to be brought under control and it's got to be brought under control on the spending side because that's what's driving it. And when you hear people say, oh, we need higher taxes. Well, first of all, higher taxes don't necessarily mean higher revenues. We've already learned that. There's a point where you raise taxes, you actually do so much damage to the economy. You bring in less revenue. We just cut taxes. And because of the positive impact that had on the economy, it's bringing in more revenues. The problem is on the spending side and that's what Congress has refused to address.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
